# A column stops being a byte (ADR-0395). Every other session here is ASCII,
# where an element, a byte and a column are the same thing and the three
# questions cannot be told apart; this is the one that separates them.
#
# `日本語` is nine bytes, three elements and six columns. So:
#
#   - the drawn row is three cells of three bytes with a null-string after
#     each, which is why six columns of text leave fourteen blanks;
#   - one Right arrow crosses one character, so the status line counts
#     1 -> 4 -> 7 in *bytes*. That is deliberate: `pascalc` reports a
#     diagnostic's column in bytes, so a byte is what Ctrl-B has to land on,
#     and the *cursor* is what goes to the column -- 1, 3, 5.
#   - Backspace removes a whole character. A byte-wise one would leave two
#     bytes that are not a character, and the undo journal would then hold a
#     fragment that is not one either, which is why this is the model's
#     business rather than the shell's.
#
# Line 3 is `e` and U+0301 COMBINING ACUTE ACCENT: three bytes, **one**
# element, one column. It is the case that tells an element from a scalar --
# a scalar-wise editor would need two Backspaces to remove it and would draw
# it in two cells.
#
# Line 4 is a family emoji, U+1F468 ZWJ U+1F469 ZWJ U+1F467: **one** element
# of eighteen bytes, which does not fit `CellMax`. Two rules meet there and
# both are visible in what it draws. Whole scalars only, so the cut takes the
# first two people and not fourteen bytes of an eighteen-byte character --
# a cut inside a UTF-8 sequence would put bytes on the terminal that are not
# a character at all. And no trailing scalar of zero width, so the joiner
# after the second person is given back too: a dangling joiner is an
# instruction to join with whatever is drawn *next*, and the cut would then
# reach into the cell beside it.
#
# The last Down is the third claim. Moving between lines keeps the *byte*,
# and byte 5 of the emoji line is the joiner in the middle of the cluster --
# so the cursor is snapped back to the element it landed inside, and the
# status line says 4:1 rather than 4:5. Without that, one Delete would take
# the joiner out of the middle of a character.
name wide.pas
size 9 20
push 日本語
push ab日
push é
push 👨‍👩‍👧
draw
esc C
draw
esc C
draw
ctrl H
draw
esc B
esc B
esc F
keys x
draw
esc B
draw
